title: Worker processes
section: Orchestration
desc: Choose between the inline pool and one Node.js process per worker, and know what is shared between them.
lede: By default bddr runs scenarios as concurrent async tasks in one process. Switch to the process pool when you want real CPU parallelism or isolation from crashes.
---
<h2>Two pools</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th><code>pool: 'inline'</code> (default)</th><th><code>pool: 'process'</code></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>How it runs</td><td>Async concurrency in one Node.js process. <code>workers</code> sets the number of lanes.</td><td>One Node.js process per lane.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Good for</td><td>API tests and small suites. Fast start.</td><td>Browser suites and CPU-heavy steps. A crash only fails one scenario.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Worker hooks</td><td>Once per run.</td><td>Once in every worker process.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory and globals</td><td>Shared by all scenarios.</td><td>Separate per process.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run --workers 4 --pool process</cb>
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">export default defineConfig({
  workers: 4,
  pool: 'process',
});</cb>

<h2>What the parent and the workers do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The parent process owns the plan, the scheduling, the reports and the <code>BeforeAll</code> and <code>AfterAll</code> hooks.</li>
  <li>Each worker loads your step files and rebuilds the same plan. bddr compares a fingerprint of the plan, so a worker that loaded different files stops the run instead of running the wrong thing.</li>
  <li>The values you set in <code>BeforeAll</code> on <code>ctx.run</code> are copied to every worker.</li>
  <li>Each worker gets the environment variables <code>BDDR_WORKER_ID</code> and <code>BDDR_WORKER_INDEX</code>. Use the index to pick a port or a test account per worker.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="diagram">
  <div class="diagram-frame"><img src="../assets/img/process-pool.svg" alt="A parent process holding the plan, scheduler, BeforeAll and AfterAll hooks and reporters sends run-scenario messages to three worker processes. Each worker holds the step files, BeforeWorker hook and browser plugin, and sends events back." width="1680" height="720" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto"></div>
  <figcaption>The parent sends scenarios out and gets events back.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Crashes</h2>
<p>If a worker process dies in the middle of a scenario, only that scenario fails and the lane starts a new process. Its <code>After</code> hooks do not run. Set <code>retry.workerCrash</code> to retry the scenario on a fresh process. If a worker cannot start at all, the run stops.</p>

<h2>Sharing data between workers</h2>
<p>Things that are per process with the process pool:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>ctx.feature</code> and <code>ctx.groups</code> stores, and module-level variables.</li>
  <li>Feature, group and rule hooks. They run once in each process that runs a member.</li>
</ul>
<p>To share a value, use <code>ctx.export</code> and <code>ctx.from</code> (see <a href="dependencies.html">Dependencies</a>), or an external system such as a database.</p>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="callout-title">Tip</p><p>Start with the inline pool. Switch to <code>process</code> when browser tests become the bottleneck or you need crash isolation.</p></aside>
